 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to prohibiting the collocation of certain facilities; prohibits the Western New York Children's Psychiatric Center from being collocated or merged with any other facility.",
 "title": "Relates to prohibiting the collocation of certain facilities",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S4630",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S4630-2017",
     "printNo": "S4630",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 195\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 4630, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to prohibiting\n      the collocation of certain facilities\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would prohibit the Office of Mental Health (OMH) from collo-\ncating or merging the Western New  York  Children's  Psychiatric  Center\n(WNYCPC), located in West Seneca, New York, with any other facility.\n \n  WNYCPC has provided high quality, comprehensive behavioral health care\nservices to children and adolescents for many years. WNYCPC was designed\nto  provide  a safe, therapeutic, family centered environment for neces-\nsary in-patient and out-patient services to children living in Allegany,\nCattaraugus, Chautauqua, Erie,  Genesee,  Livingston,  Monroe,  Niagara,\nOrleans, and Wyoming counties.\n \n  However,  the  WNYCPC  facility  is  in need of substantial repair. As\nsuch, the State sought and received public  input,  and  has  diligently\nworked with stakeholders over the past four years, to develop and imple-\nment a relocation plan that enhances community-based services, preserves\ninpatient  capacity,  and,  most  importantly, benefits the children and\nfamilies of Western New York.\n \n  Specifically, OMH announced in 2013 that  the  WNYCPC  would  relocate\nfrom  West  Seneca,  New  York,  to a new state of the art facility that\nwould be constructed on the campus of the Buffalo Psychiatric Center  in\nBuffalo,  New  York.  Construction  has  already begun and the State has\nalready invested approximately $5.1 million in the  new  facility.  This\nnew  facility  will  serve  two  purposes.  First, relocation to a fully\nmodernized campus creates an opportunity to provide the children  served\nby  WNYCPC with a facility superior to the West Seneca facility. Second,\nit will enable OMH to spend  healthcare  funding  more  efficiently  and\neffectively,  improving  mental  health  services throughout Western New\nYork.\n \n  The WNYCPC relocation enhances mental health services for children  in\nWestern New York in concrete and tangible ways:\n \n  All  inpatient  treatment,  education,  and  recreation  areas will be\nspecially designed for youth and separate from the adult services on the\nBuffalo campus. The New WNYCPC provides a myriad of barriers to  prevent\nany  interaction,  or even sight lines, between the adults and children.\nThe collocation of the New WNYCPC is consistent with how other state-op-\nerated services are provided. For example, five hospitals  already  have\nthe  same  configuration  that will be in place when the construction of\nthe New WNYCPC is completed.\n\n  No children currently served at the West Seneca facility will be moved\nto the New WNYCPC. Once the New WNYCPC is complete -  nearly  two  years\nfrom  now  - it will accept new admissions. All children being served at\n \nthe West Seneca facility will  continue  to  receive  services  at  that\nfacility until they are discharged.\n \n  The  New WNYCPC will be accessible to a broader number and demographic\nof children and families in Western New York. This is  because  children\nserved by WNYCPC predominately come from the City of Buffalo and Niagara\nCounty,  and these children will be able to remain closer to their fami-\nlies and support systems while receiving treatment. The relocation  will\nmake  it  much easier for families living in the City of Buffalo without\nan automobile, particularly low income families, to reach the New WNYCPC\nfor treatment because the New WNYCPC will be within the City of  Buffalo\nand  accessible by public transit. For families located outside the City\nof Buffalo and Niagara County, the New WNYCPC is located only 17.5 miles\naway from the West Seneca location.\n \n  The New WNYCPC is being constructed in a building that was  completely\nmodernized a few years ago, and will contain new, energy efficient heat-\ning, ventilation, air conditioning, electrical, and plumbing systems.\n \n  The  same  clinical  staff  will  transfer  to the New WNYCPC ensuring\ncontinuity of care and a proper therapeutic environment. There  will  be\nno layoffs or schedule changes for current WNYCPC employees.\n \n  The relocation will allow $3.2 million to be reinvested into child and\nfamily  mental  health  services in Western New York. Approximately $1.7\nmillion has already been pre-invested into State  and  locally  operated\nchildren's  and  family services in Western New York. Once relocation is\ncompleted, OMH will reinvest the remaining  $1.5  million  in  estimated\nsavings  into  child  and  family service programs, in consultation with\nlocal stakeholders, thereby enabling OMH to  provide  services  to  more\nthan 1,000 additional children in total as a result of the relocation.\n \n  Opponents  of  the  plan have advanced a number of arguments to oppose\nthe relocation of WNYCPC, none  of  which  have  merit.  Opponents  have\nargued that children will be co-mingled with adults in the new facility,\nthat  children will not receive proper therapeutic care, that some chil-\ndren will need to move from the current facility to  the  new  one,  and\nthat  children's  mental  health services will be reduced as a result of\nthe plan. As noted above, those claims are simply inaccurate. The  adult\nand children's facilities on the Buffalo campus will be completely sepa-\nrate,  the  new state of the art facility will enhance therapeutic care,\nno child will move from the old facility to the new one,  and  the  plan\nwill  result  in  significant  investments in community-based children's\nmental health services.\n\n  Despite these substantial, tangible benefits, the Legislature advanced\nthis bill, which effectively restricts access to mental health  care  in\nWestern  New  York, reduces investments in community based services, and\nis inconsistent with recent agreements negotiated with  the  Legislature\nduring the annual budget discussions. I cannot support a bill that would\nhave  such  a negative impact on children and families in need of mental\nhealth services and directly compromises four years of  work  that  will\ndemonstrably  provide  better  and  more  accessible  care for children.\nAccordingly, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
